::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q01::Displacement differs from distance because displacement includes {~speed =direction from start to finish ~time only ~mass}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q02::A cyclist travels 30 m east, then 10 m west. What is the displacement? {~40 m east ~20 m west =20 m east ~40 m west}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q03::The slope of a position-time graph represents {~acceleration ~force ~distance =velocity}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q04::A horizontal line on a position-time graph indicates the object is {=at rest ~accelerating ~moving at constant positive velocity ~moving backward}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q05::An object moves 120 m in 20 s at constant velocity. Its speed is {~2 m/s =6 m/s ~100 m/s ~2400 m/s}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q06::Average velocity is calculated as {~distance times time ~final speed minus initial speed =displacement divided by elapsed time ~acceleration divided by time}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q07::Which quantity is a vector? {~time ~mass ~speed =velocity}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q08::A car travels at 15 m/s for 8 s. How far does it travel? {=120 m ~23 m ~1.875 m ~7 m}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q09::Acceleration is the rate of change of {~position =velocity ~mass ~force}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q10::Velocity changes from 4 m/s to 16 m/s in 3 s. Average acceleration is {~12 m/s² ~6 m/s² =4 m/s² ~20 m/s²}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q11::The area under a velocity-time graph represents {~acceleration ~slope ~force =displacement}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q12::Constant negative acceleration appears on a velocity-time graph as {=a straight line with negative slope ~a horizontal line above zero ~a curve with increasing slope ~a vertical line}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q13::In free fall near Earth's surface, ignoring air resistance, acceleration is approximately {~0 m/s² =9.8 m/s² downward ~9.8 m/s upward ~98 m/s² downward}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q14::At the top of a vertical toss, the object's instantaneous velocity is {~equal to g ~upward =zero ~downward at maximum}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q15::For projectile motion without air resistance, horizontal acceleration is {~9.8 m/s² ~variable ~equal to vertical velocity =zero}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q16::Centripetal acceleration points {=toward the center ~away from the center ~along the tangent ~opposite gravity always}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q17::If speed doubles in uniform circular motion while radius stays constant, centripetal acceleration becomes {~twice as large =four times as large ~half as large ~unchanged}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q18::A projectile launched horizontally has initial vertical velocity {~equal to horizontal velocity ~equal to g =zero ~dependent on mass}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q19::Newton's first law describes an object's tendency to resist changes in motion, called {~friction ~weight ~acceleration =inertia}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q20::A 12 N net force acts on a 3 kg object. Its acceleration is {=4 m/s² ~36 m/s² ~9 m/s² ~0.25 m/s²}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q21::For the same net force, increasing mass causes acceleration to {~increase =decrease ~remain constant ~reverse direction}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q22::Newton's third-law force pairs act on {~the same object only ~objects with equal mass only =two different interacting objects ~objects at rest only}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q23::When net force on an object is zero, the object must {~speed up ~slow down ~be at rest =have constant velocity}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q24::Weight is calculated using {=W \= mg ~W \= ma ~W \= F/A ~W \= mv}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q25::The normal force on a level surface acts {~parallel to the surface =perpendicular to the surface ~in the direction of motion ~toward Earth's center}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q26::Static friction acts to {~increase mass ~eliminate gravity =prevent relative sliding before motion begins ~always speed an object up}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q27::Tension force is transmitted through a {~rigid surface only ~magnetic field ~fluid only =rope, string, or cable}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q28::A 25 N force right and 10 N force left produce a net force of {=15 N right ~35 N right ~15 N left ~250 N right}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q29::A free-body diagram should show {~the object's path only =all external forces acting on the chosen object ~internal forces only ~only the largest force}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q30::For two objects connected by an ideal massless rope, the rope tension is {~always zero ~greater than every applied force =the same throughout the rope ~equal to total mass}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q31::Torque magnitude depends on force and {~mass only ~speed only ~pressure only =perpendicular lever arm}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q32::An object is in rotational equilibrium when {=net torque is zero ~net force is maximum ~angular speed must be zero ~all forces act at the center}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q33::Pressure equals {~area divided by force =force divided by area ~mass times gravity ~work divided by time}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q34::A 300 N force acts over 0.75 m². The pressure is {~225 Pa ~40 Pa =400 Pa ~0.0025 Pa}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q35::Fluid pressure at a given depth increases when fluid {~area decreases ~temperature is zero ~volume decreases =density increases}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q36::Compression stress tends to {=push material together ~pull material apart ~slide layers past each other only ~remove deformation}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q37::Shear stress acts primarily {~perpendicular inward =parallel in opposite directions ~perpendicular outward ~with zero force}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q38::Isostatic uplift can occur after erosion because {~gravity disappears ~rock density becomes zero =removing mass allows crust to rise toward equilibrium ~pressure becomes independent of depth}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q39::Mountain height is limited in part by {~absence of gravity ~zero pressure at depth ~constant crustal strength =increasing stress and rock deformation at depth}
::PHY-U1-ASSESS-Q40::A strong model of mountain building should connect {=forces, material behavior, uplift, and erosion ~only a decorative cross section ~projectile range and electric current ~mass loss with zero force}

